Link <chillynois@yahoo.com> writes:

> If a wand makes an engraving vanish, then do the following steps:
> 1) Get a dagger (or any other weapon that can dull).
> 2) Engrave with the dagger until it is completely dull.
> 3) Drop the dagger on the ground.
> 4) Zap the dagger with the wand.

I wait until I'm next to a non-invisible harmless immobile or slow
monster, for example a lichen and zap it.

If I don't see the creature any more, I try to move where I saw it. 
If I can move to that sqare, I know the wand was teleportation.
If I bump into an invisible monster, I know it was make invisible. (Of
course, if I already can see invisibility, this doesn't happen. See below..)

If I DO still see the monster, I hit it. 
If I get a message about hitting an invisible monster, I know it was make
invisible (when I can see invisible).
If the message doesn't say anything about the creature being invisible, I
know the wand was cancellation.

> If the dagger does not disappear, then try engraving with it.  If you
> can engrave with it, then the wand is cancellation.  If you can't
> engrave with it, then the wand is make invisible.

Interesting, I didn't know (or had forgot) that you can't engrave with
daggers after engraving it until dullness. But I generally don't engrave
with them anyway..

